  "report": {
    "incident_title": "API returning 503s since ~20:05; possible DDoS?",
    "outcome": "conclusive",
    "summary": "The 503s are a capacity failure, not a DDoS. A marketing push ('Diwali flash sale', sent to 2.1M users at 20:00Z) drove legitimate-looking traffic about 6x above baseline (1,000 to 6,100-7,400 rps). The api HPA wanted 34 replicas but was capped at maxReplicas=12, a limit set on Sept 29 in a FinOps cost review that cut it from 40. The 12 pods saturated (CPU 93-96%), the request queue filled, and the service rejected traffic with 503s. Prometheus separately OOMed on high-cardinality campaign labels at 20:09, a second, monitoring-side failure. The traffic signature (utm_campaign=diwali_flash, timing aligned with the push, unique client IPs rising from 44k to ~400-500k) is consistent with push-driven users. No bot or WAF evidence was found, though per-source analysis was not possible. Immediate action: raise the HPA ceiling and confirm node and database capacity; do not block IPs on a DDoS assumption.",
    "root_cause": "The api HPA maxReplicas was reduced from 40 to 12 on 2026-09-29 (FinOps cost review, call-0007). When the 2026-10-02 20:00Z 'Diwali flash sale' push produced ~6x traffic, the HPA requested 34 replicas but was capped at 12 (ScalingLimited, call-0004). The 12 pods saturated and the request queue (max 200) rejected traffic with 503s (call-0017). The campaign checklist has no pre-scaling step (call-0015), and the Sept 5 campaign had already needed 31 replicas (call-0014, call-0015), so the 12-replica cap was unsafe for known campaign load.",
    "confidence": "high",
